        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-122404",
        ":question/problem": "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
