        ":question/problem": "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{zz}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+1}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
