        ":question/problem": "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2xz+{y}^{3} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{2}+x{y}^{3}-y{z}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =(2xz+{y}^{3})\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-{z}^{4})\\,j+({x}^{2}-4y{z}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
